data: {"type":"agent_chunk","agent":"researcher","content":"- CRISPR gene editing is a genetic engineering technique that modifies living organisms' genomes. It uses a simplified version of the bacterial CRISPR-Cas9 antiviral system [1].\n- The Cas9 nuclease, paired with a synthetic guide RNA, is delivered into a cell to cut the genome at a chosen location [1].\n- Genome editing inserts, deletes, modifies, or replaces DNA at site-specific locations, using programmable molecules that create double-strand breaks [3].\n- In nature, CRISPR is a DNA sequence family in bacteria and archaea, built from bacteriophage DNA and used to detect and destroy similar phage DNA [2].\n- A Hacker News post headlined a CRISPR breakthrough as opening the door to treating a broad array of diseases [4]. A separate post reported that HIV in cell culture was eliminated using CRISPR-Cas editing [5]."}

